<p>If you just found a bottle of ZaZa, Tianaa, or Pegasus in your loved one&rsquo;s car or bedroom, you&rsquo;re probably confused and scared. It came from a gas station or a smoke shop, so how bad can it be? The honest answer: tianeptine addiction is real, it behaves a lot like opioid addiction, and the withdrawal can be brutal. Families often discover it late because the product looks like a harmless supplement.</p>
<p>You&rsquo;re not overreacting, and you&rsquo;re not alone. More families are calling me about tianeptine every month, and most had never heard the word until it was a crisis in their home. Here&rsquo;s what it is, why it&rsquo;s so addictive, the warning signs, and what you can actually do to help.</p>

<h2>What Is Tianeptine and Why Is It Called Gas Station Heroin?</h2>
<p>Tianeptine is a drug sold in some countries as a prescription antidepressant, but in the United States it is not approved by the FDA for any medical use. Instead, it shows up in gas stations, smoke shops, and online stores packaged as a &ldquo;dietary supplement&rdquo; or &ldquo;nootropic&rdquo; under names like ZaZa Red, Tianaa, Neptune&rsquo;s Fix, and Pegasus. People call it gas station heroin because, at the doses people actually take, it activates the same opioid receptors in the brain that heroin and oxycodone do.</p>
<p>That last part is what catches families off guard. At low, prescribed doses overseas, tianeptine works mostly as an antidepressant. But people chasing the effect quickly escalate to ten, twenty, even a hundred times that amount. At those doses, it produces an opioid-like high, opioid-like tolerance, and opioid-like withdrawal. The FDA has issued repeated warnings about it, and a growing number of states have banned it, but it remains easy to buy in much of the country.</p>
<p>The packaging is the trap. When something sits next to energy drinks and phone chargers, the brain files it under &ldquo;basically safe.&rdquo; That&rsquo;s how people who would never touch heroin end up physically dependent on tianeptine.</p>

<h2>What Are the Warning Signs of Tianeptine Addiction?</h2>
<p>The most common warning signs of tianeptine addiction are empty red-and-white pill bottles, frequent trips to gas stations or smoke shops, mounting spending, and flu-like sickness that appears whenever the person goes a few hours without a dose. Because tianeptine leaves the body quickly, withdrawal can start within hours, so the person&rsquo;s day begins to revolve around the next bottle.</p>
<p>Here&rsquo;s what families typically notice:</p>
<ul>
<li>Bottles or foil packets labeled ZaZa, Tianaa, Pegasus, or Neptune&rsquo;s Fix appearing in cars, trash cans, or pockets</li>
<li>Spending that doesn&rsquo;t add up &mdash; a serious habit can cost hundreds of dollars a week, one small bottle at a time</li>
<li>&ldquo;Flu&rdquo; symptoms that come and go: sweating, chills, nausea, diarrhea, restlessness, and anxiety that vanish after they run an errand</li>
<li>Nodding off, slurred speech, or pinpoint pupils, especially after they&rsquo;ve been out to the store</li>
<li>Irritability, panic, or desperation when they can&rsquo;t get to a gas station</li>
</ul>
<p>If several of these sound familiar, trust what you&rsquo;re seeing. Families almost always sense the problem before they can name it. If you are also noticing <a href="/blog/kratom-addiction-warning-signs-families">kratom addiction warning signs</a>, the two often show up in the same household.</p>

<h2>Why Is Tianeptine Withdrawal So Hard to Quit Alone?</h2>
<p>Tianeptine withdrawal looks and feels like opioid withdrawal, and for many people it hits faster and harder. Because the drug is short-acting, users may dose every few hours just to feel normal, and stopping abruptly can bring on days of vomiting, body aches, insomnia, crushing anxiety, and depression. That&rsquo;s the engine that keeps this addiction running: people aren&rsquo;t chasing a high anymore, they&rsquo;re running from being sick.</p>
<p>This is why &ldquo;just stop buying it&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t work as a plan. Most people dependent on tianeptine have already tried quitting on their own. They white-knuckle it for a day, the withdrawal becomes unbearable, and the gas station is five minutes away. Willpower loses that math almost every time.</p>
<p>The good news is that treatment works. Medical detox can manage tianeptine withdrawal safely and far more comfortably, and many programs now have experience with it because cases have climbed sharply in recent years. Some people also need help for the depression or anxiety they were quietly trying to medicate in the first place &mdash; treating both together matters.</p>

<h2>How Can Families Help a Loved One Addicted to Tianeptine?</h2>
<p>Start by taking it seriously, saying what you see, and refusing to fund it &mdash; while offering a real path to treatment. Your loved one may lean hard on the fact that tianeptine is &ldquo;legal&rdquo; and &ldquo;just a supplement.&rdquo; Don&rsquo;t argue the label. Talk about what you&rsquo;re watching: the sickness, the money, the fear, the person you&rsquo;re losing.</p>
<p>A few practical steps:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Get informed before you talk.</strong> Read up on tianeptine so you can&rsquo;t be dismissed with &ldquo;it&rsquo;s not a drug.&rdquo; You now know it acts on opioid receptors &mdash; that&rsquo;s the conversation.</li>
<li><strong>Pick a calm moment, not a crisis.</strong> Speak with love and without lectures. &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve noticed you get sick every morning until you go to the store. I&rsquo;m scared for you&rdquo; lands better than accusations.</li>
<li><strong>Stop enabling the supply.</strong> Don&rsquo;t give cash, don&rsquo;t run the errands, don&rsquo;t look away from the bottles. It feels harsh; it&rsquo;s actually love with a spine. If this is where you get stuck, read more on <a href="/blog/enabling-loved-one-addiction">how to stop enabling a loved one&rsquo;s addiction</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Line up help before the conversation.</strong> Know which detox or treatment programs handle tianeptine so that when your loved one says yes &mdash; even for a moment &mdash; you can move immediately.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you&rsquo;ve tried all of this and hit a wall of denial, that&rsquo;s not your failure. It&rsquo;s the addiction doing what addictions do. It&rsquo;s also the point where a professionally guided family intervention changes the odds, because it brings structure, preparation, and a unified family voice to a conversation that keeps going sideways. Here is <a href="/blog/what-is-an-intervention">what to expect during a professional intervention</a>.</p>

<h2>There Is a Way Through This</h2>
<p>Tianeptine addiction thrives on the same thing every addiction does: secrecy, shame, and a family that feels too overwhelmed to act. You&rsquo;ve already broken the first rule by learning what you&rsquo;re dealing with. People do recover from this &mdash; I&rsquo;ve watched families walk out the other side stronger than they went in. The road starts with one honest conversation and a plan behind it.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Tianeptine Addiction</h2>

<h3>Is tianeptine legal in the United States?</h3>
<p>Tianeptine is not FDA-approved for any use in the U.S., and a growing list of states has banned it outright. In states without a ban, it is still sold in gas stations and smoke shops as a so-called supplement, which is why availability varies so much by location.</p>

<h3>Is tianeptine really like heroin?</h3>
<p>At the high doses people misuse, tianeptine activates the same mu-opioid receptors as heroin and prescription painkillers. That&rsquo;s why it produces a similar high, similar tolerance, and similar withdrawal &mdash; and why the nickname gas station heroin is earned, not hype.</p>

<h3>How long does tianeptine withdrawal last?</h3>
<p>Acute tianeptine withdrawal typically peaks within the first few days and eases over roughly a week, though anxiety, insomnia, and depression can linger longer. Because symptoms can be severe and start within hours of the last dose, medically supervised detox is the safest way to get through it.</p>

<h3>Can you overdose on tianeptine?</h3>
<p>Yes. High doses can cause dangerous sedation and slowed breathing, much like an opioid overdose, and some products have been found mixed with synthetic cannabinoids or other unlisted drugs. If someone is unresponsive or breathing abnormally, call 911 &mdash; naloxone (Narcan) may help and is worth administering while you wait.</p>

<h3>What are ZaZa and Tianaa?</h3>
<p>ZaZa and Tianaa are two of the most common brand names for tianeptine products sold in gas stations and smoke shops. They&rsquo;re marketed as supplements or mood boosters, but they contain the same drug driving the addiction described in this article.</p>

<h3>Does an intervention work for someone addicted to tianeptine?</h3>
<p>Yes. A professional intervention works the same way for tianeptine as it does for opioids or alcohol: it helps the family present a loving, unified, prepared message with treatment ready to go. It&rsquo;s often the turning point for someone who insists the product is harmless because it&rsquo;s legal.</p>

